# Notes App Backend

A secure Flask REST API for a personal notes application. Users can sign up, log in, and manage their own private notes. Session-based authentication ensures each user can only view, create, update, or delete their own notes.

## Description

This backend was built as a Flask summative lab covering authentication, database relationships, and RESTful API design. Every user has their own set of notes (one-to-many relationship between `User` and `Note`), and all note endpoints are protected so that a logged-in user can never see or modify another user's data.

## Tech Stack

- Flask
- Flask-SQLAlchemy
- Flask-Migrate
- Flask-Bcrypt (password hashing)
- Faker (database seeding)
- SQLite

## Installation

1. Clone the repository:
   ```
   git clone <your-repo-url>
   cd notes-app-backend
   ```
2. Install dependencies:
   ```
   pipenv install
   pipenv shell
   ```
3. Set up the database:
   ```
   flask db upgrade
   ```
   (If migrations haven't been initialized yet: `flask db init`, then `flask db migrate -m "initial migration"`, then `flask db upgrade`.)
4. Seed the database with sample data:
   ```
   python seed.py
   ```

## Running the App

```
python app.py
```

The API will run at `http://localhost:5555`.

## Authentication

This API uses **session-based authentication** (cookies). Log in once and the session cookie authenticates subsequent requests — no token needs to be sent manually.

## Endpoints

### Auth

| Method | Endpoint | Description | Auth Required |
|---|---|---|---|
| POST | `/signup` | Create a new user account | No |
| POST | `/login` | Log in and start a session | No |
| GET | `/check_session` | Return the current logged-in user | Yes |
| DELETE | `/logout` | End the session | Yes |

### Notes

| Method | Endpoint | Description | Auth Required |
|---|---|---|---|
| GET | `/notes?page=1&per_page=10` | List the current user's notes, paginated | Yes |
| GET | `/notes/<id>` | Get a single note (must belong to the user) | Yes |
| POST | `/notes` | Create a new note (`title`, `content`) | Yes |
| PATCH | `/notes/<id>` | Update a note's `title` and/or `content` | Yes |
| DELETE | `/notes/<id>` | Delete a note | Yes |

Any request to a `/notes` endpoint without a valid session returns `401 Unauthorized`. Any request for a note that belongs to another user returns `404 Not Found` (so users can't tell whether a note exists at all).

## Testing with Postman

1. Set the base URL to `http://localhost:5555`.
2. Enable "Automatically follow cookies" (Postman does this by default within one workspace) so your session cookie persists between requests.
3. `POST /signup` with a JSON body: `{"username": "yourname", "password": "yourpassword"}`.
4. `POST /login` with the same credentials.
5. Call `GET /notes`, `POST /notes`, `PATCH /notes/<id>`, `DELETE /notes/<id>` — the session cookie from step 4 authenticates them.
6. Try logging in as a second user and requesting the first user's note `id` directly — it should return `404`, confirming access control works.
7. `DELETE /logout`, then try `GET /check_session` — it should return `401`.

## Sample Seeded User

After running `python seed.py`, check the console output for a sample username; the password for all seeded users is `password123`.